diff --git a/packages/api/internal/sandbox/reservations/redis/README.md b/packages/api/internal/sandbox/reservations/redis/README.md
--- a/packages/api/internal/sandbox/reservations/redis/README.md
+++ b/packages/api/internal/sandbox/reservations/redis/README.md
@@ -11,10 +11,20 @@
 
 ## Flow
 
-`Reserve` runs a Lua script that atomically removes stale pending entries, checks whether the sandbox already exists or has already pending start, enforces the team limit using `SCARD(storage index) + ZCARD(pending zset)`, deletes any stale result key, and adds the sandbox ID to the pending zset.
+`Reserve` runs a Lua script that atomically removes stale pending entries, checks whether the sandbox already exists or has already pending start, enforces the team limit using `SCARD(storage index) + ZCARD(pending zset) + HLEN(restore reservations)`, deletes any stale result key, and adds the sandbox ID to the pending zset.
 
 When creation completes, it removes the sandbox from the pending zset, writes a TTL result key containing either the sandbox or the creation error, and publishes the routing key.
 
 A waiter subscribes to the routing key, probes the result key immediately, then waits for PubSub notifications or the 1 second fallback ticker. PubSub is best-effort; the fallback ticker is required for correctness.
 
 `Release` is called when the sandbox is removed from storage (`Store.Remove`). It removes the sandbox from the pending zset, deletes the result key, and publishes the routing key.
+
+Prepared restores also use an operation-bound hash at
+`sandbox:storage:{teamID}:reservations:restores`. It has no stale TTL: an API
+worker disappearing does not prove its VM stopped. Both admission paths count
+this hash atomically with the running index and ordinary pending starts. A
+restore retry with the same operation retains its reservation; another operation
+cannot release it. The restore worker releases it only after registering the VM
+or receiving a host-confirmed never-started cleanup result. Registration followed
+by a lost release reply can temporarily count twice; recovery retries the same
+release instead of admitting excess work.
